  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Seafood Cluster</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project will focus on efforts to influence Asian seafood companies to adopt human rights/worker rights policies and practices throughout the supply chain. This will build upon the pressure from EU legislation and the most recent German supply chain act, as well as successes under the previous iteration of this initiative. This involves strengthened partnership and coordination of CSOs in Southeast Asia to engage with the private sector in ensuring that worker rights are protected. A significant aspect of this effort will be further bolstering the CSO Coalition for Ethical and Sustainable Seafood (CSO Coalition), which has membership from worker rights organizations and small -scale fisheries, as well as international NGOs in the platform. Another key highlight has been engendering better understanding and capacity among CSOs regarding HRDD, including the launch of a policy brief on the HRDD landscape in Asia and maintaining ongoing engagement with Asian companies regarding HRDD. Another approach for this project is to advance this work on the ground through strengthening coordination among civil society organizations (CSOs) in three countries and allowing Oxfam to further inhabit its niche promoting an enabling policy environment for inclusive and responsible supply chains through HRDD influencing toward Asian companies. The project will benefit from the coordination and convening capacity of Oxfam in Asia, which will oversee the work in Thailand and the two Oxfam Indonesia and Vietnam country offices. Across the focus area, we aim to explore linkages and cross -learnings with businesses and NGOs in South Asia. At the same time, Oxfam is concerned that many other companies, despite improved knowledge, are not taking significant steps to adopt HRDD into their seafood operations. As such, under this proposal, there are fewer activities around campaigning and more on collaborating with companies to adopt HRDD, while using tools to influence laggard companies to adop</narrative>
  </description>
